    {
      "id": 18,
      "title": "",
      "viz": "text",
      "grid": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 9,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 12
      },
      "content": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; color: var(--text-color, #333); background-color: var(--bg-color, #fafafa); padding: 12px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n    \n    <p><h4 style=\"color: var(--heading-color, #4db6ac); margin-bottom: 10px;\">What this chart shows:</h4>\n    This chart displays both the <span style=\"color: var(--highlight-color, #81c784); font-weight:bold;\">percentage</span> and \n    <span style=\"color: var(--highlight-color, #81c784); font-weight:bold;\">number</span> of test cases in the selected \n    <span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">Regression cycle</span>, grouped by team and categorized into three types:\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px;\">\n        <li><strong>Automated:</strong> Tests that run automatically without human involvement.</li>\n        <li><strong>Manual:</strong> Tests that require a person to execute.</li>\n        <li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Unit-test-like functional tests that validate against a reference database.</li>\n    </ul>\n    </p>\n    \n    <p><h4 style=\"color: var(--heading-color, #4db6ac); margin-bottom: 10px;\">Why it matters:</h4>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px;\">\n        <li>Helps us understand testing efficiency and workload distribution across teams.</li>\n        <li>A higher Baseline and Automated percentage often indicates a healthier quality engineering process and reduced risk.</li>\n        <li>Important to note: some manual tests are intentionally not automated due to complexity or business needs.</li>\n        <li>A higher number of manual tests can mean more effort and longer regression cycles.</li>\n    </ul>\n    \n    <p><h4 style=\"color: var(--heading-color, #4db6ac); margin-bottom: 10px;\">How to interpret:</h4>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px;\">\n        <li>Higher percentages often (but not always) reflect more effective teams in delivering features.</li>\n        <li>100% Automated + Baseline means no regression and low risk for that team.</li>\n        <li>Example: If a team shows <strong>120 Automated</strong> and <strong>5 Manual</strong>, it means they mostly rely on automation but still perform some manual checks and might need couple of hours to wrap each iteration.</li>\n    </ul>\n\n</div>\n",
      "mode": "html"
    },
