    {
      "id": 20,
      "title": "",
      "viz": "text",
      "grid": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": 55,
        "w": 24,
        "h": 7
      },
      "content": "<div style=\"font-family: Arial, sans-serif; color: var(--text-color, #333); background-color: var(--bg-color, #fafafa); padding: 12px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n\n    <p><h4 style=\"color: var(--heading-color, #4db6ac); margin-bottom: 10px;\">What these charts show:</h4>\n    These two charts illustrate how test cases are distributed across different test cycles for both \n    <span style=\"color: var(--highlight-color, #81c784); font-weight:bold;\">manual</span> and \n    <span style=\"color: var(--highlight-color, #81c784); font-weight:bold;\">automated</span> executions. \n    Each cycle represents a specific phase or focus area in the testing process.</p>\n    \n    <p><h4 style=\"color: var(--heading-color, #4db6ac); margin-bottom: 10px;\">Why it matters:</h4>\n    Understanding this distribution helps us see how tests are spread across various functional or risk-based cycles. \n    This ensures that critical areas receive the right level of attention during regression and hardening phases.</p>\n    \n    <p><h4 style=\"color: var(--heading-color, #4db6ac); margin-bottom: 10px;\">How to interpret:</h4>\n    Since we follow a <span style=\"color: var(--warning-color, #ffb74d); font-weight:bold;\">risk-based testing approach</span>, \n    different tests are executed in different cycles based on their importance and risk level. \n    For example, high-risk features might appear more in hardening cycles, while broader coverage happens during regression.</p>\n    \n</div>",
      "mode": "html"
    },
